                'body' => [
                    'en' => '<h2>Start with the workflow, not the feature list</h2><p>Write down who uses the system, what each person records, which approvals are required, what management must see and which external systems exchange data. A long feature list is not useful if the software cannot represent the actual workflow.</p><h2>Define the controls that cannot be optional</h2><ul><li>Roles and permissions for sensitive actions.</li><li>Approval rules for payments, purchases or operational decisions.</li><li>Audit history for important changes.</li><li>Reliable backups and a documented restore process.</li><li>Reports that reconcile with the underlying records.</li></ul><h2>Confirm localization before signing</h2><p>If the organization needs English, Dari or Pashto, verify the actual interface. Also define currency handling, date formats, right-to-left behavior, branch structure and the documents users need to print or export.</p><h2>Choose deployment based on operating reality</h2><p>Cloud, desktop, offline-first and hybrid systems solve different problems. Identify which tasks must continue during connectivity loss, how many branches need shared data and who owns backups and updates.</p><h2>Compare proposals on the same scope</h2><p>Give shortlisted vendors the same requirements and ask for modules, implementation stages, deployment, migration, training, support and exclusions. This makes the comparison more meaningful than headline price alone.</p>',
